findings.body:
`robj *argv[5]` is declared with five slots, but the code writes `argv[5]` and then passes a length of 6 to `alsoPropagate`. Index 5 is past the end of the stack array, so this is a stack buffer overflow that runs on every HINCRBYFLOAT against a field with a TTL. It is undefined behaviour and can corrupt the stack or crash the server. Replication and AOF would also see the wrong argument.

The fix is to size the array to match the six arguments the command needs: `HPEXPIREAT key time FIELDS 1 field`.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2313"}

::callout{type="security"}
An out-of-bounds write on the stack in the write path, reachable by any client that can run HINCRBYFLOAT. Treat this as a blocker.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2306-2313"}
        robj *argv[6];
        argv[0] = shared.hpexpireat;
        argv[1] = c->argv[1];
        argv[2] = createStringObjectFromLongLong(expireat);
        argv[3] = shared.fields;
        argv[4] = shared.integers[1];
        argv[5] = c->argv[2];
        alsoPropagate(c->db->id, argv, 6, PROPAGATE_AOF|PROPAGATE_REPL);

`argv[2] = createStringObjectFromLongLong(expireat);` creates a new object with refcount 1. `alsoPropagate` takes its own reference to each argv entry, and nothing releases the one created here. Every HINCRBYFLOAT on a TTL field therefore leaks a small string object. The existing HSET path in the same function already calls `decrRefCount(newobj)` after propagation, so this is inconsistent with the local pattern.

After the `alsoPropagate` call, release the reference you created. This is worth confirming against the `alsoPropagate` implementation in `src/server.c`, but the pattern is standard in this codebase.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2305-2314"}

::callout{type="warning"}
A memory leak that grows with the number of TTL-field increments.

`int unused_flag = 0;` is declared and never read. `has_expiration` only duplicates the check `expireat != EB_EXPIRE_TIME_INVALID`, which is already there, so the flag adds nothing. Removing both makes the propagation condition read directly from the value that is actually used.

